--csharp-head
--csharp
    static string At(long gas, long slice) {
        var rt = new Rt();
        rt.gasLimit = gas; rt.sliceEnd = slice;
        RtRefreshCheckpoint(rt);
        return rt.checkpoint == long.MaxValue ? "none" : rt.checkpoint.ToString();
    }
    static string Set(long limit, long slice) {
        var rt = new Rt();
        rt.sliceEnd = slice; rt.gasTrips = 7;
        RtSetGasLimit(rt, limit);
        return rt.checkpoint + "/" + rt.gasTrips;
    }
    public static void Main() {
        System.Console.WriteLine(At(0, 0) + " " + At(100, 0) + " " + At(0, 50) + " "
                                 + At(100, 50) + " " + At(50, 100) + " | "
                                 + Set(200, 50) + " " + Set(10, 50));
    }
--expect
none 100 50 50 50 | 50/0 10/0
--expect-why
DERIVED from "zero means unlimited, and the checkpoint is the nearer of the
two budgets", not recorded from a run.

THE FIRST THREE ROWS ARE THE WHOLE TEST, and a plain minimum fails all three.
`min(0, 0)` is 0, `min(100, 0)` is 0 and `min(0, 50)` is 0 -- "stop
immediately" -- where the contract says an unset budget cannot stop anything.
So the answers are `none`, then 100, then 50. Verified by injecting exactly
that: all three targets agreed on `0 0 0 50 50` and rows four onwards stayed
right, so those rows alone would have passed it.

`none` RATHER THAN A NUMBER, and this is the point of the case. "Nothing is
counting" is spelled `u64::MAX` on native and `Long.MAX_VALUE` on both ports,
2^64-1 against 2^63-1, and each runtime tests against ITS OWN -- `rt.rs:1007`
is `self.checkpoint != u64::MAX`. Converging the literal was tried first and
is a BUG: native would write a checkpoint its own `counting()` reads as a live
budget, so an unbounded guest would take the counting loop. The toy therefore
asks each host whether the checkpoint is ITS sentinel, which is the property
that has to agree.

Rows four and five pin the minimum in BOTH directions, so a comparison written
backwards answers 100 for one of them. They say nothing about the zero rule,
which is why they are not first.

`50/0` and `10/0` are `rt-set-gas-limit`: the limit lands, the checkpoint is
re-armed against whichever budget is nearer -- the slice at 50, then the new
limit at 10 -- and `gas_trips` goes from 7 to 0. Seeded at 7 rather than 0 so
that "the clear did nothing" and "the clear worked" are different answers.
